---
title: Angular library
description: The GateKeeper Angular package — a public SPA client with a signal service, provider, and route guard.
section: Application libraries
---

`@gatekeeper/angular` is the Angular (2+) integration for GateKeeper. It wraps the
framework-agnostic `@gatekeeper/spa-oauth` public client in a signal-based `GateKeeperService`, a
`provideGateKeeper()` provider, and an `authGuard` route guard.

The package source lives at `packages/gatekeeper-angular`; a runnable example is at
`frontends/angular`. The package is consumed **from source** (no Ivy/`ng-packagr` build) — the
example app's `tsconfig` `paths` point at `packages/gatekeeper-angular/src/public-api.ts`, so the
app compiler builds it.

## Requires a public OAuth client

Angular SPAs run in the browser, so they must use a **public** OAuth client:

- `client_type: "public"` — **no client secret**.
- PKCE S256 is the only client protection.
- Tokens live in `sessionStorage` and are exposed to XSS by design.
- Add the app origin to the server's `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`.

Prefer a confidential BFF whenever the framework has a server runtime.

## Provide the client

```ts
import { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { provideRouter } from '@angular/router';
import { provideGateKeeper, type GateKeeperSpaConfig } from '@gatekeeper/angular';
import { AppComponent } from './app/app.component';

// Angular has no Vite import.meta.env; edit these or wire them to environment.ts.
const config: GateKeeperSpaConfig = {
	idpBaseUrl: 'http://localhost:8080',
	authUiBaseUrl: 'http://localhost:5173',
	tenantId: '',
	clientId: '',
	redirectUri: 'http://localhost:5177/',
	scope: 'openid profile email'
};

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
	providers: [provideRouter([]), provideGateKeeper(config)]
});
```

`provideGateKeeper()` registers the config token, the service, and an `APP_INITIALIZER` that runs
`handleRedirectCallback()` on boot so a `?code=` return is finished before the app renders. Only the
public `clientId` is configured — no secret.

## Read state from the signal service

`GateKeeperService` exposes Angular signals, so templates update automatically:

```ts
import { Component, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { GateKeeperService } from '@gatekeeper/angular';

@Component({
	standalone: true,
	template: `
		@if (gk.isAuthenticated()) {
			<p>Signed in as {{ gk.user()?.email ?? gk.user()?.sub }}</p>
			<button (click)="gk.signOut()">Sign out</button>
		} @else {
			<button (click)="gk.signIn()">Sign in</button>
		}
	`
})
export class AccountComponent {
	gk = inject(GateKeeperService);
}
```

The service exposes the `snapshot`, `status`, `isAuthenticated`, `user`, `expiresAt`, and `error`
signals, plus `signIn()`, `signOut()`, `refresh()`, `getAccessToken()`, and `fetchUserInfo()`.

## Guard protected routes

```ts
import { authGuard } from '@gatekeeper/angular';

export const routes = [
	{ path: 'account', canActivate: [authGuard], loadComponent: () => import('./account') }
];
```

`authGuard` allows navigation when authenticated; otherwise it kicks off `signIn()` and blocks the
route.

## Fetch userinfo

```ts
const claims = await this.gk.fetchUserInfo(); // GET /oauth/userinfo?tenant_id=... (Bearer)
```

## Example app

`frontends/angular` is a standalone Angular 18 SPA (dev port 5177). Register a public client with
`frontends/angular/scripts/register-public-client.sh`, set the values in `src/gatekeeper.config.ts`,
add `http://localhost:5177` to `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`, then `npm install && npm start`.
